-- ============================================================================
-- PD4 PROFESSIONAL DEEP SEED: Rod Pump Design, intermediate tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: THE CARD. The design IS the card. Everything a beam
-- pump is judged on, the plunger stroke, both polished rod loads, the work per
-- cycle and therefore the power, and the oil that reaches the tank, is a
-- property of the dynamometer card the wave makes, and the card exists only
-- after a march. So this tier is where a rod pump design stops being
-- arithmetic and starts being physics.
--
-- THE TIER IS BUILT ON ONE DISAGREEMENT, AND BOTH SIDES OF IT ARE GRADED IN
-- THIS TIER RATHER THAN SPLIT ACROSS TWO. Sp = S - Fo*Er, the spring rule, is
-- the first equation in every rod pump text: the plunger loses exactly the rod
-- stretch. It is a STATIC rule, and it knows nothing about a rod string that
-- is still moving when the polished rod turns round. The damped wave equation
-- says the plunger travels FURTHER than the spring rule allows, by about a
-- tenth of what the spring rule itself predicts on the capstone well, which is
-- 8.92 percent of the surface stroke, and the whole of that difference is
-- inertial OVERTRAVEL. A learner who stops at the spring model
-- gets the stretch right and the stroke wrong, in the same sitting, which is
-- the point of grading them together.
--
-- A CLIFF IN THE CODE, AND THE CAPSTONE SITS THREE THOUSANDTHS ABOVE IT.
-- runRodPumpDesign warns `incompleteFillage` at fillage strictly below 0.85.
-- A barrel that fills 0.853 is fifteen percent short of full and raises NO
-- warning at all. The same well at 0.849 raises it, and the two differ in
-- production by 0.4959 bbl/d, which is to say the warning is a statement about
-- a threshold and not about the pump. Reading a warning list rather than a
-- number is the error this tier exists to make expensive.
--
-- WHAT THE TIER MUST NOT OVERSTATE, and it is handed straight to the tier
-- above. The two polished rod loads this tier reports are what the DESIGN
-- REPORTS. Whether they are what the march COMPUTED is a different question,
-- and it is the Expert tier's opening result. This tier is graded on the
-- report, at the engine's shipped defaults, because that is what a studio user
-- receives.
--
-- The only number that travels from this tier to the next is the card itself,
-- as an object. The tier above restates every condition from the raw geometry.
-- ============================================================================

values ('rodpump', 'intermediate', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-the-card-is-the-design","title":"The Card Is the Design","lesson_keys":["l01-what-a-dynamometer-card-is","l02-a-wave-in-a-rod-string","l03-what-the-march-computes","l04-what-a-card-asserts"]},{"key":"m02-stretch","title":"Stretch","lesson_keys":["l01-static-stretch","l02-the-spring-rule","l03-overtravel","l04-the-size-of-the-disagreement","l05-which-one-is-the-stroke"]},{"key":"m03-the-loads","title":"The Loads","lesson_keys":["l01-peak-polished-rod-load","l02-minimum-polished-rod-load","l03-the-load-range","l04-what-moves-the-loads","l05-what-a-load-is-measured-against"]},{"key":"m04-power","title":"Power","lesson_keys":["l01-polished-rod-horsepower","l02-the-card-area","l03-what-power-costs","l04-what-power-does-not-include"]},{"key":"m05-what-the-well-makes","title":"What the Well Makes","lesson_keys":["l01-fillage","l02-displacement-against-production","l03-a-cliff-in-the-code","l04-the-effective-factor","l05-what-production-cannot-be-read-from"]},{"key":"m06-the-professional-reading","title":"The Professional Reading","lesson_keys":["l01-the-story-so-far","l02-working-the-capstone","l03-onward"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
